 },
 "note": "A real walk of FamilySearch collection 2152685, Italy, Pola and Trieste, Catholic Church Records, kept as a fixture because it is the case this atlas exists for and the matcher must never silently stop handling it. Its tree runs Province > Comune or Frazione > Parish, where Province is Pola (Pula, Croatia), the comuni are Verteneglio (Brtonigla, Croatia), Buie (Buje, Croatia) and Isola d Istria (Izola, Slovenia), and the Parish level holds CHURCH DEDICATIONS rather than settlements. Every trap in one collection. It also carries five single-level Croatian paths — Davor, Bale, Čabar, Borojevići, Topolo — each a Croatian village this shelf has not drawn whose name also exists in Bosnia, Montenegro or Slovenia. Without them the MUST-NOT half of the test could never fire, which is not a test. And five Brazilian municipalities whose names are shared across the Americas — Califórnia, Colorado, Flórida, Belém, Buenos Aires — each filed under a municipality of the same name, which is how one name counted as two witnesses and put 300 books on US states and Argentine provinces. Two of the Brazilian rows use TWO DIFFERENT shared names — Colorado > Flórida, Buenos Aires > Santa Cruz — so the distinct-name rule is satisfied and only the filing group can refuse them. Without those the Brazil case passed for the wrong reason and proved nothing."
}